grow - extend a filesystem over the whole of its volume

  grow hd0p3

A card written from the system image carries a 16 MiB /home whatever the
card's size. This makes the filesystem cover the rest of its partition.

A larger volume needs a larger allocation table, and the table sits in
front of the data, so the data moves forward by as much as the table
grows. Cluster numbers do not change, so no file is rewritten.

Losing power during the move leaves the volume unreadable. FAT has no
journal and there is no way around that. Copy anything that matters off
the volume first.

Refused while the volume is mounted, when the filesystem already fills
its volume, and when the larger volume would need a wider FAT than it
has: growing does not change the width, because that would renumber every
cluster.
